# commodities
#
# 1 LLM token corresponds very roughly to 3 characters. There are four types:
# input tokens:        user prompts, anything new that wasn't cached
# cache create tokens: system prompt, file content, tool output, message history
# cache read tokens:   history being reread from cache on every turn
# output tokens:       generated by the AI
#
# Some assumptions.
# Simplest rough measure of AI's work: output tokens
# Best rough measure of energy use: output + 0.5 * cache create
# Thinking effort is reflected in the token counts (more thinking = more tokens).
# Model is not reflected in token counts (smarter models' tokens use more energy than dumber models).
# We currently record just generic output tokens, mostly from opus and fable, as t (or kt).
#
# Conservation tips.
# Output tokens are the most significant for energy use. (Cache create tokens are next.)
# Tokens are easily wasted: check/minimise context (MCPs, memory, long chat histories), and reduce effort.
# Or switch to a dumber model, with cheaper tokens.
# But the dominant environmental lever is the grid mix powering the datacenter, not the raw token count.
# The same model on hydro-powered infrastructure vs. coal differs by ~10× on carbon.
#
# Some ballpark conversions.
# 1 (opus) output token =~
#  10 joules
#  0.0028 Wh
#  0.05 mL water
#  Lifting a 1 kg bag of sugar 1 meter
#  A 10W LED running for 1 second (or a 100W bulb for 1/10 s)
#  A laptop running for ~0.2 seconds at typical 50W load
#  ~1/700th of an AA battery (a fully charged AA stores ~7 kJ)
#  ~1/5,000th of an iPhone battery charge (~50 kJ)
#  ~1/200th of a kitchen match (a match releases ~2 kJ when burned)
#  Heating one drop of water by ~50 °C (a drop is ~50 mg; 10 J takes it from room temp to nearly boiling)
#  ~10 human heartbeats' worth of mechanical work (heart does ~1 J/beat)
#  ~0.0024 food calories (a single raisin is ~4 kcal = ~17 kJ, so one token ≈ 1/1,700 of a raisin)
#  Roughly 1/30th of a traditional Google search (older estimates put a search at ~300 J, though modern numbers are lower)

# prices for general conversions

P 0000-01-01 opus_output_tokens $0.000075
P 0000-01-01 opus_output_tokens 10 j
P 0000-01-01 opus_output_tokens 0.0028 Wh
P 0000-01-01 opus_output_tokens 0.05 mL
P 0000-01-01 opus_output_tokens 10 heartbeats
P 0000-01-01 opus_output_tokens 0.000588 raisins
P 0000-01-01 opus_output_tokens 1 led_secs
P 0000-01-01 opus_output_tokens 1 kgm
P 0000-01-01 opus_output_tokens 0.0000066667 prius_miles
P 0000-01-01 opus_output_tokens 0.001 g

# commodity aliases for units that should be converted to another by default

commodity $1.

commodity 1,000.0 kt            ; generic kilotokens
  ; alias: opus_output_tokens
  ; alias: 1000 t
  ; alias: 0.001 Mt
  ; alias: 0.000001 Gt
  ; alias: 3000 chars
commodity 1,000.  opus_output_tokens
commodity 1,000.  t
commodity 1,000.0 Mt
commodity 1,000.0 Gt
commodity 1,000.  chars

commodity 1,000.0 kWh           ; kilowatt hours of energy
  ; alias: 1e3 Wh
  ; alias: 1e-3 MWj
  ; alias: 1e-6 GWj
commodity 1,000.0 Wh
commodity 1,000.0 MWh
commodity 1,000.0 GWh

commodity 1,000.0 kj            ; kilojoules of energy
  ; alias: 1e3 j
  ; alias: 1e-3 Mj
  ; alias: 1e-6 Gj
commodity 1,000.j
commodity 1,000.0 Mj
commodity 1,000.0 Gj

commodity 1,000.0 L             ; litres of water
  ; alias: 1e3 mL
commodity 1,000. mL

commodity 1,000. heartbeats     ; human heartbeats
commodity 1,000.0 raisins       ; the energy in 1 raisin
commodity 1,000.0 prius_miles   ; miles in a prius
commodity 1,000. led_secs       ; a 10-watt LED running for 1 second
commodity 1,000. kgm            ; 1 kilogram lifted 1 meter
commodity 1,000.0 kg            ; kilograms of CO2 emission
  ; alias: 1e3 g
commodity 1,000. g
